/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (IV. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION A. Experimental Setup The models were trained using the TensorFlow/Keras framework on an NVIDIA A100 GPU. We used the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 1e-4 and a ReduceLROnPlateau callback. The loss function was categorical_crossentropy. Due to the high memory demands of 3D data, we used a batch  size of 4.B. Performance Comparison We compared our DPHF model against three baseline models to validate our design: 1. Baseline 3D CNN: One single larger 3D CNN with the 4 modalities stacked as input channels \(early fusion\). 2. Parallel 3D CNNs - Path 1 Only: Our Specialized-Feature Path, where the 4 vectors are concatenated and then fed straight to the classifier. 3. TD-CNN-LSTM \(Path 2 Only\): Our Fused-Feature Path as described in [6] is fed directly to the classifier. The results on the test set are summarized in Table I. TABLE I. COMPARATIVE PERFORMANCE OF MODELS | Model | Test Accuracy \(%\) | Test F1-Score \(Macro\) | Precision \(Macro\) | Recall \(Macro\) | |---|---|---|---|---| | Standard 3D CNN [9] | 97.1% | 0.970 | 0.971 | 0.970 | | Parallel CNNs \(Path 1\) [5] | 98.5% | 0.985 | 0.985 | 0.986 | | TD-CNN-LSTM \(Path 2\) [6] | 98.9% | 0.989 | 0.990 | 0.989 | | DPHF \(Ours\) | 99.2% | 0.992 | 0.992 | 0.992 | Our DPHF model outperformed the rest according to all metrics. This result is statistically significant. It shows that while the "fused-feature" path \(TD-CNN-LSTM\) is slightly more powerful than the "specialized-feature" path \(Parallel CNNs\), combining them yields a real and measurable accuracy increase. The model is clearly leveraging unique information from both streams.)
/E (IV. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION A. Experimental Setup The models were trained using the TensorFlow/Keras framework on an NVIDIA A100 GPU. We used the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 1e-4 and a ReduceLROnPlateau callback. The loss function was categorical_crossentropy. Due to the high memory demands of 3D data, we used a batch  size of 4.B. Performance Comparison We compared our DPHF model against three baseline models to validate our design: 1. Baseline 3D CNN: One single larger 3D CNN with the 4 modalities stacked as input channels \(early fusion\). 2. Parallel 3D CNNs - Path 1 Only: Our Specialized-Feature Path, where the 4 vectors are concatenated and then fed straight to the classifier. 3. TD-CNN-LSTM \(Path 2 Only\): Our Fused-Feature Path as described in [6] is fed directly to the classifier. The results on the test set are summarized in Table I. TABLE I. COMPARATIVE PERFORMANCE OF MODELS | Model | Test Accuracy \(%\) | Test F1-Score \(Macro\) | Precision \(Macro\) | Recall \(Macro\) | |---|---|---|---|---| | Standard 3D CNN [9] | 97.1% | 0.970 | 0.971 | 0.970 | | Parallel CNNs \(Path 1\) [5] | 98.5% | 0.985 | 0.985 | 0.986 | | TD-CNN-LSTM \(Path 2\) [6] | 98.9% | 0.989 | 0.990 | 0.989 | | DPHF \(Ours\) | 99.2% | 0.992 | 0.992 | 0.992 | Our DPHF model outperformed the rest according to all metrics. This result is statistically significant. It shows that while the "fused-feature" path \(TD-CNN-LSTM\) is slightly more powerful than the "specialized-feature" path \(Parallel CNNs\), combining them yields a real and measurable accuracy increase. The model is clearly leveraging unique information from both streams.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [0]
>>
